        explanation:
          "Now, turn from analysis to the first real tool a state has available. Deterrence works by convincing an adversary that the costs of taking a given action will genuinely outweigh whatever benefits it might gain, but only if the underlying threat is credible: the adversary has to believe you hold both the capability and the actual political will to follow through on it [3]. Deterrence comes in two related forms worth keeping separate, deterrence by denial, making the action itself unlikely to succeed, and deterrence by punishment, making the cost of even a successful action unacceptably high, and a well-designed deterrent posture usually combines both rather than relying on either one alone. You should also distinguish general deterrence, the ongoing background effort to prevent any challenge from ever arising in the first place, from immediate deterrence, the sharper, more urgent effort to stop a specific, already-signaled threat from actually being carried out, since the two call for genuinely different tools and different levels of urgency.",
