        whyItMatters:
          "Alliances solve a genuinely real problem, since no single state can realistically deter every threat it faces entirely alone, but they also create their own distinctive risks. The most important of these is entrapment, where a weaker ally's own reckless behavior can drag a considerably stronger partner into a conflict it never actually chose, simply because credibility requires honoring the underlying commitment once it has been made [4]. The mirror-image risk, abandonment, cuts the other way: an ally that doubts a partner's commitment may act more aggressively on its own, believing correctly or not that help will not actually arrive when it is genuinely needed. Managing both risks at once, staying credible enough to deter abandonment while staying cautious enough to avoid entrapment, is one of the hardest ongoing balancing acts in alliance management. You should also recognize that alliance treaties are themselves deliberately written with varying degrees of ambiguity, and a treaty's drafters sometimes leave the exact scope of a commitment genuinely unclear on purpose, preserving some flexibility for the stronger partner while still supplying enough credible reassurance to hold the alliance together in ordinary times. Reading a treaty's actual text closely, rather than assuming it means whatever the alliance's public reputation suggests, is therefore a genuinely essential step before you advise anyone on what a given commitment actually obligates its members to do.",
