          "Now, turn to the constraint every negotiator ultimately has to satisfy twice over. Any international negotiation is really a two-level game: a leader has to reach an agreement acceptable to the other state at the international table while simultaneously securing an outcome that can actually survive ratification, formal or informal, back home [6]. A deal that looks entirely rational internationally can still collapse if the leader's own legislature, coalition partners, or domestic public will not actually accept it, and a skilled negotiator treats that domestic constraint as a genuine, binding limit on what they can agree to, not merely as an excuse to bring up after the fact. Putnam's own term for this domestic constraint is the win-set, the full range of agreements that would actually be ratified at home, and a negotiator's entire international bargaining position is shaped by how large or small that win-set genuinely is. You should also note that a win-set is not fixed; skilled leaders actively work to expand their own win-set at home before or during a negotiation, building coalitions and shaping public opinion so that a deal genuinely acceptable at the international table has a real chance of actually surviving ratification once it gets there.",
