    overview:
      "Welcome to domestic policy. This field studies how a government actually turns a political goal into a functioning program: a budget, a set of incentives, and an agency capable of carrying it out, that then has to survive contact with a legislature, courts, a bureaucracy that does not always do what it is told, and public opinion that keeps moving underneath all of it. Over the next forty minutes you will work through ten ideas in the order a policy itself actually moves. We start with the overall map, the stages every policy cycle passes through, and the sharper question of why an issue's moment on the agenda opens when it does and closes just as fast. We then ask how quickly policy actually changes once that window opens, and what it becomes once you account for the different levels of government, and the frontline officials, who actually have to carry it out. We turn next to the two forces that decide whether an idea with real merit still fails to become law: who bears the costs and who reaps the benefits, and which interest groups can be brought along. We then look at how a policy, once made, quietly rewrites the politics around itself, and at the tool a frustrated executive reaches for when the legislature will not move at all. We close where the cycle itself closes, asking whether any of it actually worked. Keep one question running underneath everything you hear: whose incentives does this actually change, and will the answer still hold once the program meets the real world.",
