    connections:
      "Ten ideas, one recurring test: given the actual decision rule this race will be decided by, does this activity move a vote that will actually change the outcome. The invisible primary and the primary-general tradeoff are the structural facts a candidate is operating under before most voters are even paying attention, and the fundamentals set the baseline the entire rest of the campaign is fighting at the margins of. Targeting, message and framing, and polling are how a campaign reads the race and allocates its effort and its voice within that margin, while earned and paid media are the two channels that message actually has to travel through to reach anyone at all. Resource allocation applies all of that to the actual electoral map, and opposition research and rapid response defend and attack within the resulting message battle. Get-out-the-vote closes the loop, converting everything covered before it into the only thing that actually counts on election day, a vote cast. As you follow any live campaign from here forward, try running it through all ten ideas in order: place the candidate's invisible-primary standing, name the positioning tension they are navigating, check the fundamentals, and ask whether the tactical choices you are actually watching, an ad, a debate answer, a canvassing push, are moving a vote that matters under the race's real decision rule or simply generating activity that feels like campaigning without actually being it.",
    source: "claude",
