    connections:
      "Ten ideas, one recurring test: can you actually count to the threshold that matters here, and what is the cheapest way to get there. Pivotal politics is the structural map of what is even achievable before anyone negotiates anything, and agenda control is the gatekeeping power that decides which bills ever reach a vote within that map at all. Vote counting tells you whether you currently have the numbers, and logrolling and heresthetic are the two tools for getting there when you do not, one by trading across unrelated priorities, the other by restructuring the choice itself. Procedural leverage determines which path, and which threshold, a bill is actually moving against, while BATNA and constituent-pressure management describe the leverage and the dual audience every negotiator is managing throughout. Conference committees and veto bargaining are the two gates a bill still has to clear even after it first passes, one reconciling two chambers into a single text, the other surviving the president's own signature. As you follow any live legislative fight from here forward, try running it through all ten ideas in order: place the relevant pivot, check whether gatekeeping ever let the bill reach a vote at all, read the whip count, and ask what threshold is actually being cleared at each stage, since a bill's headline public debate is very often the least informative part of the actual process deciding whether it becomes law.",
